<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.3.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01784</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.3.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>f524df07-7b2c-4689-8cf0-9d386177b611</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="a86be7e9-a521-420f-bc87-0699ce61b7bf" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para>Sketch or describe the figure in Step 4 and Step 15.
          
          <figure><media alt="Three steps of a growing pattern"><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_05_10_01.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para> How many small squares are in Step 4? </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Step 4 is a 4-by-4 array of squares with the lower-left corner square moved to the upper-right corner.
      </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.3.2 and 7.3.3. 
      </para>
            <para>The correct answer is 16.
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="1efcfb52-3163-409c-870a-e78eba258ba3" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para> How many small squares are in Step 15?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Step 15 is a 15-by-15 array of squares with the lower-left corner square moved to the upper-right corner.
      </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.3.2 and 7.3.3. 
      </para>
            <para>The correct answer is 225.
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="bae33e1e-93fa-45e0-80cf-05879f2e5c72" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> Which equation represents the relationship between the step number, \(n\), and the number of small squares, \(y\), in each step?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(y=n^2\)</para>
            <para>\(y=n^2+1\)</para>
            <para>\(y=n^2-1\)</para>
            <para>\(y=n^2-n\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(y=n^2\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.3.2 and 7.3.3. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(y=n^2\).
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="75564220-9969-4f6b-8201-1514dbe3b2c4" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Each figure is composed of large squares and small squares. The side length of the large square is \(x\). Choose the correct expression that represents the area, \(y\), of the shaded part of each figure.</para>
            <para> Figure A 
            
            <figure><media alt="Large square with 1 by 1 small squares removed from each corner."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_05_10_02.svg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(y=x^2\) </para>
            <para>\(y=x^2-1\)</para>
            <para>\(y=x^2-4\)</para>
            <para>\(y=x^2-8\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(y=x^2-4\) The figure is an \(x\)-by-\(x\) square with 4 unit squares that aren’t shaded.
      </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.3.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(y=x^2-4\). The figure is an \(x\)-by-\(x\) square with 4 unit squares that aren’t shaded.
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="f4885315-c632-49bd-8ced-821d528863a3" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Figure B
            
            <figure><media alt="2 large squares side by side. On the side of the right square, there are 2 small squares stacked vertically, each with dimensions 2 by 2."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_05_10_03.svg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(y=x^2\)</para>
            <para>\(y=2x^2+8\)</para>
            <para>\(y=x^2+8\)</para>
            <para>\(y=2x^2\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(y=2x^2+8\). There are two squares that are \(x\) by \(x\), and there are smaller squares that are 4 units each added to the figure.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.3.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(y=2x^2+8\). There are two squares that are \(x\) by \(x\), and there are smaller squares that are 4 units each added to the figure. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="00953db5-9afb-4be6-92c1-ef559312ee4d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <newline/>
            <para>Use the sequence below to answer questions a and b.</para>
            <para> How many squares are in Step 5? </para>
            <para> Enter your answer here: </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Every step has one more row and column than the step number, and then one square is taken away.
      </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.3.3 and 7.3.4. 
      </para>
            <para>The correct answer is 35.

      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="5fe140cf-4801-41b8-a65b-577282d9e5db" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> Choose the correct expression that represents the area, \(y\), of the shaded part of each figure for \(n\) steps.</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(n-1\)</para>
            <para>\(n^2-1\)</para>
            <para>\((n+1)^2\)</para>
            <para>\((n+1)^2-1\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \((n+1)^2-1\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.3.3 and 7.3.4. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is \((n+1)^2-1\).
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
